WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.920
Bats are more like us
than you would think,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.720
because they live a really long time

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.760
and that’s really unusual
for a mammal of that size.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.840
So a mammal of that size
usually lives one to two years

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.600
and bats can live up to 40 years
which is amazing.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.720
They have a really interesting repair
mechanism for their DNA,

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.880
so they can repair all the DNA that
is broken and they never get cancer.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.720
Bats are very resilient animals.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.520
They also have
a number of adaptations

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.360
that make them very good at
surviving in harsh environments.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.040
Bats use loads of different senses.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.560
So bats use their eyes,
so they're not blind,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.720
but they also use
sense of smell.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.960
So they have big smell
regions of their brains

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.400
when they need to find food.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.800
The primary sense
that many bats use

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.560
is echolocation,
or sonar in human terms.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.560
Although the bats have been using
these signals for 50 million years,

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.640
we've been using them
for maybe 100 years.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.520
We also need to be
operating on our senses.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.760
We have to operate on our
gut feeling a lot of the time.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.800
Jungles are probably the best place

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.920
for having to use
your senses constantly

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.640
because they're hot,
they're humid, dark.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.880
So you're almost feel a bit
like a bat, very claustrophobic.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.520
And you have to be aware
of the little changes.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.480
So when things go quiet, potentially
there is a predator in the area.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.760
Bats, very often,
live in very large colonies.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.160
Very often these colonies contain
millions of individuals.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.320
One of the largest aggregations
of mammals on the planet

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.520
is a bat roost
in Texas in Bracken Cave.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.400
We don’t really know
how many there are

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.360
but it's between 10 and 20
million bats that live there.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.040
This colonial way of life
has several advantages.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.360
So bats can keep each other warm

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.920
and reduce energy costs
by huddling together.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.840
Another advantage of being in a group

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.680
is it reduces your individual risk
of being eaten by a predator.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.440
So bats live in large communities,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.080
they have to get on
with lots of other people

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.240
and on expedition
we can be in a large team.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.760
So building relationships,
sustaining those relationships,

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.880
developing systems
for living in a community,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.040
generally away from normal
society, bit like the bats,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.520
and therefore we have
to develop our own ways

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.560
of supporting each other,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.920
looking after each other,
teamwork,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.200
using the collective mind
to be aware of danger

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.720
or to enable us to actually do
the tasks that we're going to do.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.640
Bats don’t just care about
their own blood relatives,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.040
they care about other individuals
in their wider community too.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.560
They form these buddy pairs

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.840
and if you don’t find
a meal for that night

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.360
you can ask your buddy to share
what they have for that night

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.760
and then the next night
you can reciprocate.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.760
So it's called reciprocal altruism,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.200
one of the very few examples
of reciprocal altruism

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.320
in mammals in the entire world.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.160
So bats are often covered in quite
large numbers of parasites.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.160
The bats get rid of parasites,
in part, by grooming.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.720
Not only do they
groom themselves

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.640
but very often they do
something called allogrooming

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.920
where individuals
groom one another

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.160
proving social bonds among
individuals in colonies.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.240
Bats are well known
for their personal hygiene,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:27.440
their levels of personal hygiene.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.480
The dark, dank bits need to be
cleaned on a regular basis,

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:32.600
and also you need to be aware

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.600
of your cleanliness,
particularly around other people.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:35.880
It can ruin relationships,

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.720
particularly if you're sharing a
tent with them for a period of time,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.120
and your ability to continue
doing the expedition.

